Subject: [PATCH] dtl: hand batches to the worker thread without semaphores

The immediate context records DDI calls into batches that a worker thread
replays. Upstream hands each batch over with two Win32 semaphores and a
mutex-guarded std::deque, and SubmitBatchIfIdle polls the consumed
semaphore with a zero timeout every ten recorded commands while any batch
is outstanding. On Windows those are cheap kernel calls. Under Wine every
semaphore operation is a wineserver round trip unless esync/msync is
active, so the game's recording thread pays an IPC every ten commands,
another per submitted batch, and one per batch drained on every flush,
map and synchronous destroy.

Replace the handoff with the single-producer/single-consumer rings and
address waiter from relay_batch_ring.hpp:

  * m_QueuedBatches and m_FreeBatches become bounded rings of owning
    pointers. The outstanding count is the distance between the queue's
    indices, so IsBatchThreadIdle is two loads, not a system call.
  * Each side sleeps only when there is genuinely nothing to do (worker
    on an empty queue, recording thread on a full one or in
    WaitForBatchThreadIdle), through WaitOnAddress, which Wine serves
    without the server. A notify with nobody asleep is a fence and a load.
  * SubmitBatch now finishes and queues a batch in one m_RecordingLock
    scope. Every submitter already serialized on that lock, so the queue
    has a single producer, and a batch ID below m_RecordingBatchID is
    always in the queue when SyncWithBatch looks for it.
  * m_SubmissionLock shrinks to an SRW lock, m_SyncLock, held only by
    SyncWithBatch and by the worker while it picks up and retires a
    batch. It keeps upstream's guarantees: SyncWithBatch sees a stable
    queue and m_CompletedBatchID, a flush mask it ORs into a queued batch
    is read at retire, and a PrepForCommandQueueSync done on an empty
    queue finishes before the worker touches the immediate context again.
    The worker's own flush stays inside the lock, before the slot is
    released, as upstream ordered it.
  * PostSubmitCallback still runs on the recording thread, now from
    DrainCompletions whenever that thread sees the worker retire a batch.
  * RetireBatch runs from post-batch functions, so on the worker when
    there is one, which makes the worker the free ring's only producer.
    A free ring that is full deletes the retired batch instead.
  * Shutdown sets an exit flag and wakes the worker, which leaves once
    the queue is empty, instead of posting a semaphore to an empty queue.

The translation layer now imports WaitOnAddress/WakeByAddressAll from
api-ms-win-core-synch-l1-2-0.dll, so link it with -lsynchronization.
Command recording, non-worker ProcessBatch and deferred contexts are
unchanged.

